<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ōmi Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ōmi Province was an old province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, corresponding to modern Shiga prefecture, surrounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-biwa">Lake Biwa</a>. Lying on the main routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> to the east and north, it was strategically vital and fought over during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> by clans such as the Rokkaku, Kyōgoku and Azai. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> built his Azuchi castle there from 1576.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/omi-province.jpg" alt="Ōmi Province" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Omi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ōmi Province was one of the provinces of the Tōsandō circuit and covered the same land as modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiga-prefecture">Shiga Prefecture</a>. Under the Engishiki classification it ranked as one of the 13 great countries in importance and one of the near countries by distance from the imperial capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. It bordered Wakasa and Echizen to the north, Mino and Ise to the east, Iga and Yamato to the south, and Yamashiro and Tanba to the west. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-biwa">Lake Biwa</a>, the largest lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, lies at its centre. The name comes from older words for a &quot;freshwater sea&quot;, with the written form 近江 fixed after the Taiho Code of 701 and a decree of 713.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been settled since at least the Yayoi period, and in the Kofun period it appears to have been dominated by powerful immigrant clans, notably the Wani clan from Baekje. Emperor Tenji built the Ōmi Ōtsu Palace in what is now Ōtsu and ruled from it from 667 until 672, issuing the Ōmi Code. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinshin-war">Jinshin War</a> then broke out among his children, with several battles fought in Ōmi. In 745 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a> declared the residence he had built at Shigaraki in 740 the capital, but abandoned it the same year because of the expense. In 788 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saicho">Saichō</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enryaku-ji">Enryaku-ji</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a>.</p><p>The Sasaki clan held the post of military governor from the late Heian into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period, and its branches, the Rokkaku and Kyōgoku, dominated the province into the Muromachi period. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> northern Ōmi was a battleground between the Azai and Asakura, while the Rokkaku had the support of the Kōka ikki. In the late 1560s and early 1570s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> invaded and defeated the Azai, Asakura, Rokkaku and Kōka and, in 1573, the Ashikaga shogunate. He built Azuchi Castle in Ōmi.</p><p>After his death on 21 June 1582 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> awarded much of the province to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> it was divided into domains, the largest being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikone">Hikone</a>, ruled by the Ii clan, and five of the 53 stations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokaido">Tōkaidō</a> and eight of the 69 of the Nakasendō lay there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>, Ōtsu Prefecture and Nagahama Prefecture were created in November 1871 and merged in January 1872 to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiga-prefecture">Shiga Prefecture</a>, which covers the old province. Between 1876 and 1881 it also included the Reinan region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui-prefecture">Fukui Prefecture</a>, which gave it a shoreline on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a>, but local inhabitants strongly opposed the merger and it was withdrawn.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ōmi Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Azai were retainers of the Kyogoku governors of northern <b>Omi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/azai-clan-domain">Azai clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
